#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065343 :tutorial/content "When simplifying an algebraic expression involving square roots, it's useful to remember that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|.\n$$\n\nIt's tempting to think that $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is equivalent to $x,$ but that's not true because $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is always non-negative, even if $x$ is negative.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate $\\sqrt{x^2}$ for a couple of different values of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16}=4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn both cases, using absolute value gives the correct result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}}=|4| & =4 & ✓ \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}}=|-4| & =4 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e09a82d-1598-56ed-9888-9a1bfa380f55" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1604 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
